  "worked_solution": "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\u0394x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+j\u0394x$ with $\u0394x=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\u0394x=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\u0394x & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+j\u0394x)\u0394x \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{n+4j}{n}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{n}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(n+4j)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(n)].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
